# Hostile self-audit — parallel stopping-face train-pair cut

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Audited source:** `target_c_parallel_train_pair_cut_2026-09-12.md`  
**Disposition:** PASS at the stated conditional scope; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This audit checks the proposed exclusion of two disjoint same-direction complete
trains joining the same two bounded stopping faces. It does not extend the result to
different train directions or to the outer face.

## 1. Train disjointness

The canonical train construction partitions contact-edge occurrences by their
unoriented projective direction. Distinct complete trains in one direction therefore
have disjoint edge sets and disjoint midpoint paths. The canonical narrow-cone
monotonicity also rules out a crossing of two same-direction train paths. The report
states disjointness explicitly, so the cut proof remains valid even if a future train
normalization changes how this fact is registered.

## 2. Jordan closure

Bounded nonquadrilateral faces are strict convex polygons with empty open interiors.
The two terminal midpoints on each face can therefore be joined by an interior arc
that meets the contact graph only at its endpoints. For two disjoint arcs connecting
the same pair of faces, the endpoint pairing induced by their planar order gives a
simple four-piece Jordan curve. The train paths meet the contact graph only at the
midpoints of their own parallel contact edges. Hence the closed curve has no
unlisted contact crossings.

All train-path rhombi and both bounded faces lie in the convex hull of the full source,
so the Jordan curve and its interior are contained in that hull. No source vertex is
used as a point on a closing arc.

## 3. Diameter rows

An active diameter endpoint is an extreme point of the full source convex hull. An
extreme point cannot lie in the interior of a Jordan domain contained in that convex
hull. Therefore every active diameter endpoint is outside the cut shore. This uses
only the exact diameter-endpoint extremality argument and does not assume that the
positive-contact core contains all source points.

## 4. Contact rows and rigidity

The closed curve intersects contact edges only in the two selected trains, and every
such edge is parallel to their common direction `u`. The piecewise constant velocity
`Ju` inside the shore and zero outside has zero first variation on every same-shore
contact and on every crossed contact because `u dot Ju=0`. Active diameter rows have
both endpoints outside and also have zero first variation.

The field is nonzero on a core vertex because a train edge is crossed. It is not a
Euclidean infinitesimal motion: the canonical Section-57 endpoint count supplies at
least three active diameter edges in distinct projective directions and therefore at
least two distinct core vertices outside the shore. A Euclidean infinitesimal motion
vanishing at two distinct points is zero. This contradicts the canonical mixed-rank
identity `rank R_{C union H}[V]=2|V|-3`.

## 5. Scope controls

- Contact-isolated source points are retained and assigned zero velocity.
- A common endpoint face would be a bounded loop, already excluded by the global
  endpoint-multigraph theorem; the present audit assumes two distinct bounded faces.
- Different-direction trains between the same faces are not covered: their cut edges
  need not admit one transverse translation.
- An outer-face endpoint is not covered because a closing arc through the unbounded
  face need not remain inside the source convex hull, so the diameter-endpoint step
  would require a separate argument.
- No source-preserving finite diameter decrease is inferred; the contradiction is
  infinitesimal mixed rigidity on the nonisolated core.

**Final audit verdict:** PASS as a conditional proof-only structural lemma. The
endpoint multigraph still has unresolved different-direction pairs and outer-face
repetitions, and C-II remains open.
